<html xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word"><head><meta charset=utf-8><title>Karen Lochhead - Full Pack Part 6</title></head><body><h1>Karen Lochhead - Full Pack Part 6</h1><p>Karen Lochhead — Full Notes &amp; Transcripts (Part 6)</p><p>Compiled from Plaud. Includes meeting notes and full transcripts. Being looked at and reviewed.</p><p>06-10 System Troubleshooting</p><p>Plaud file id: fb017c5735f9e13793cdb810b3834aa4</p><p>Notes</p><p>### Summary</p><p># System Troubleshooting and Workflow Optimisation</p><p>A senior colleague provides comprehensive, hands-on technical and operational support to Karen Lochhead, resolving specific system errors related to insurance quotes and joint mortgage applications. The session transitions into a strategic discussion on optimising platform workflows, managing complex protection cases, analysing client demographics, and implementing a formal support structure to alleviate Karen&#x27;s stress and ensure regulatory compliance.</p><p>---</p><p>## Technical Troubleshooting for Protection and Mortgage Applications</p><p>During the initial phase of the call, Bob Duncan assists Karen in diagnosing and resolving two primary technical roadblocks. The first issue involves a discrepancy in a life insurance quote via Aviva, where the coverage unexpectedly jumped from £180,000 to £194,000. Bob Duncan explains that this is caused by the provider&#x27;s minimum premium requirements (e.g., a £6.71 or £7 threshold); because the clients are relatively young, the only way to meet the minimum premium is to artificially inflate the cover amount. Karen is advised to accept this adjustment and justify it in her suitability report.</p><p>The second issue pertains to a joint mortgage borrower proprietor application for Mark Dobie and his student daughter, Neve. The Halifax system threw multiple errors, including issues with additional property options, empty loan amounts, and invalid employment statuses. Bob Duncan guides Karen through unlocking the case, changing the residential status to &quot;home mover&quot;, setting Neve&#x27;s status to a student, and adding Mark&#x27;s £12,000 private pension income to clear the errors and secure the Decision in Principle.</p><p>---</p><p>## Platform Workflow and Client Management Process</p><p>Following the technical resolutions, the conversation shifts to reinforcing the correct procedural workflow within the new platform. Bob Duncan provides a step-by-step walkthrough of managing a client case post-Decision in Principle. This involves confirming Electronic Identity Verification (EIDV) scans, setting the basic fee structure (e.g., £300), and generating the mortgage passport, which automatically emails the client their agreement and requested documents.</p><p>Bob Duncan heavily emphasises the importance of delegating administrative tasks to the support team (Megan). Tasks such as managing Communitas returns, requesting credit reports, and uploading documents should be systematically pushed to admin. Furthermore, Bob Duncan stresses that transferring a case to the Midas system should only occur after the platform data—including the fact find and budget planner—is entirely complete, preventing the need for tedious manual re-keying.</p><p>---</p><p>## Performance, Stress, and Implementation of a Formal Support Plan</p><p>Addressing Karen&#x27;s apparent burnout and feelings of being overwhelmed, Bob Duncan outlines a formal support plan designed to protect both Karen and the business from regulatory risk. Acknowledging that Karen has historically been a strong adviser, Bob Duncan notes that the current chaotic workflow needs correction.</p><p>Upon returning from holiday in two weeks, Victoria will initiate a structured support programme. This will involve fortnightly meetings with Karen to review her diary, assess her caseload, and ensure she is properly delegating administrative tasks. The ultimate goal is to help Karen rebuild professional habits, allowing her to focus strictly on providing advice, adding compliance notes, and issuing suitability letters, thereby significantly reducing her stress levels.</p><p>---</p><p>## Lead Generation, Client Mix, and Data Analysis</p><p>The discussion broadens to strategic business metrics and the quality of leads Karen receives. Bob Duncan clarifies a recent issue with the West End booking link, noting that Megan had accidentally been reallocating appointments due to a backend system update, which has now been fixed to ensure a better distribution of first-time buyer opportunities.</p><p>Bob Duncan shares company-wide data revealing that their average client is an early-career professional with a combined household income between £107,000 and £113,000 (with Karen&#x27;s clients often reaching up to £191,000). This demographic is highly primed for insurance products. Bob Duncan explains that this data is crucial for pushing back against high-level management (such as Alan or Bob), who often view performance metrics in a vacuum without understanding the nuance of client mix, local competition, or the difference between purchase and remortgage opportunities.</p><p>---</p><p>## Navigating Complex Protection Cases and Regulatory Obligations</p><p>The final segment addresses the severe administrative burden of complex protection cases, particularly for older clients or those with underlying health issues. Karen expresses intense frustration over scenarios like her client Martin McCree, whose premium was rated by 100% due to diabetes and high blood pressure, leading to months of chasing GP reports and blood tests only for the client to potentially decline the policy. She also mentions Charlotte, whose application was declined due to a pending smear test.</p><p>Bob Duncan reassures Karen that from a regulatory standpoint, her primary obligation is simply to deliver the advice. If an underwriter applies a 200% rating and the client refuses to pay, the regulatory duty has still been fulfilled. To mitigate the frustration of these &quot;wasted&quot; efforts, Bob Duncan reiterates that the new platform includes specific &quot;touch points&quot; that automatically assign tasks to admin staff to chase GP reports and medical updates, deliberately removing Karen from the administrative crossfire.</p><p>---</p><p>## Action Items</p><p>**@Karen Lochhead**</p><p>- [ ] Edit the Aviva quotation on the quote screen to match the minimum premium requirement. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Add a file note justifying the premium change and update the suitability report. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Change Mark Dobie&#x27;s status to &quot;home mover&quot; on the Halifax site. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Put a private pension income of £12,000 for Mark Dobie into the Halifax system. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Save the application changes in the Halifax system. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Send Mark and Neve Dobie a list of required documents via the platform. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Submit the Communitas form to admin. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Transfer the client&#x27;s case to Midas only after the fact find and budget plan are completely finished. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Call the pending client and speak to him. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Call Bob Duncan back after the client meeting. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Fix the four outstanding cases. - [Tomorrow]</p><p>- [ ] Sit down with Victoria to review diary management, case loads, and delegate admin work. - [TBD]</p><p>**@Bob Duncan**</p><p>- [ ] Message Megan to call clients and inform them of a 10-15 minute delay. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Unlock Mark Dobie&#x27;s case to address missing loan amount information. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Submit Mark Dobie&#x27;s application after fixing errors. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Confirm EIDV scans for Mark and Neve Dobie. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Set fees for Mark Dobie&#x27;s case (basic two for £300). - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Update Mark Dobie&#x27;s status to &quot;actively looking&quot; and edit the callback date to Friday. - [TBD]</p><p>**@Megan / Admin Team**</p><p>- [ ] Phone Bob Duncan&#x27;s 2:00 PM appointment to inform them of a 10-15 minute delay. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Mark the &quot;commenters&quot; task as &quot;returned&quot;. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Review uploaded client documents and notify the adviser of any missing files. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Request credit reports if a case declines. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Request updated payslips or GMP statements if required. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Upload client documents to the platform. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Update the MAP spreadsheet. - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Chase GP reports and bring the answers back to the adviser (e.g., Martin McCree&#x27;s case). - [TBD]</p><p>**@Victoria**</p><p>- [ ] Meet with Karen fortnightly (starting in two weeks) to review cases, monitor diary management, and enforce correct platform habits. - [TBD]</p><p>### 0611_1459_Based on a</p><p>### Support Plan</p><p>Full transcript</p><p>Karen Lochhead: Hi, how you doing? Good, how are you?</p><p>Speaker 2: I&#x27;m good. I&#x27;m phoning on your That question.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: Yes, right, but they both solution builder as well.</p><p>Speaker 2: But they got sent me back a question.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: I didn&#x27;t look at it because I&#x27;ve just had my head done up time times. But, it&#x27;s it&#x27;s all becoming a bit weird Odd for me right in the sense of nothing&#x27;s, working and I don&#x27;t know why my foot, my, my, my camera isn&#x27;t working either. Tell me, if you can see me, right.</p><p>Speaker 2: I can see myself.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: Can you see You can see me now, Kenya. This morning.</p><p>Speaker 2: Did you How, did you find it?</p><p>Karen Lochhead: I was a lot of screaming women actually talking a lot of shaid. They, they were talking aboutum how would you add uh building and contents? And how would you do something else? And I went, no, I&#x27;ve enough to be getting on with clearing this list.</p><p>Speaker 2: That&#x27;s fair enough then, but okay to say they just jump off of that. I didn&#x27